{%- comment -%}
  snippets/lada-size-table.liquid, the Lada measurement table and its
  inches/centimetres toggle, as one reusable component.

  WHY IT IS A SNIPPET AND NOT SECTION MARKUP. The table has to appear
  twice: once as the lada-sizing section, and once inside a size-guide
  popover sitting immediately beside the size selector on the PDP, which
  is where the doubt actually happens. Two copies of the same <table>
  drift apart the first time someone edits one of them, and the second
  copy is the one nobody remembers to update. So the markup lives here
  and both entry points render it. The section owns the heading, the
  drawing and the WhatsApp route; this file owns nothing but the table.

  EVERY STRING IS A PARAMETER WITH A DEFAULT, so the PDP call site can
  be one line and still ship correct English, while the section passes
  the client's edited settings straight through.

    {% render 'lada-size-table', uid: 'pdp-size' %}

  PARAMETERS
    uid            unique id stem. REQUIRED in spirit: two instances on
                   one page share a radio `name` if they share a uid,
                   and then the popover's toggle drives the section's
                   table. Defaults to 'lada-size' for single use.
    rows           '38R,38,32,29,16.5,15|40R,...' one row per pipe,
                   cells comma separated, INCHES AS BARE NUMBERS.
    columns        header labels, comma separated. The first is the size
                   column; the rest are measurements.
    caption_in     caption shown while the table is in inches.
    caption_cm     caption swapped in by the toggle.
    note           optional line under the table.
    units_legend / unit_in_label / unit_cm_label   the toggle's strings.
    units          pass false to render the table with no toggle.

  INCHES ARE IN THE MARKUP, CENTIMETRES ARE COMPUTED. The page has to be
  usable with JavaScript off, and a shopper in Sheikhupura reading a
  chart of empty cells is worse served than one reading inches he has to
  convert himself. So every cell ships its inch value as text AND as
  data-lada-in, the conversion is arithmetic the client never maintains
  by hand, and a second column of hand-typed centimetres cannot fall out
  of step with the first. The toggle itself carries `hidden` and is
  revealed by the script, because a control that does nothing is worse
  than no control.

  data-lada-in holds the value verbatim, including any stray unit the
  client types ("38 in"), so switching back to inches restores exactly
  what was entered rather than a re-rounded number.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign uid = uid | default: 'lada-size'
  assign col_src = columns | default: 'Size,Chest,Waist,Length,Shoulder,Collar'
  assign cols = col_src | split: ','
  assign last_col = cols.size | minus: 1
  assign row_src = rows | default: '38R,38,32,29,16.5,15|40R,40,34,29.5,17,15.5|42R,42,36,30,17.5,16|44R,44,38,30.5,18,16.5|46R,46,40,31,18.5,17'
  assign row_list = row_src | split: '|'
  assign cap_in = caption_in | default: 'All measurements in inches, taken on the body.'
  assign cap_cm = caption_cm | default: 'All measurements in centimetres, taken on the body.'
  assign legend = units_legend | default: 'Units'
  assign in_label = unit_in_label | default: 'Inches'
  assign cm_label = unit_cm_label | default: 'Centimetres'

  assign show_units = true
  if units == false
    assign show_units = false
  endif
-%}
<div class="lada__sizetable" data-lada-sizetable
     data-cap-in="{{ cap_in | escape }}" data-cap-cm="{{ cap_cm | escape }}">

  {%- if show_units -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      A real radio group in a real fieldset. Two states that are mutually
      exclusive and that both stay visible is what a radio group is for;
      it arrives keyboard-operable, arrow-key navigable and correctly
      announced without a line of ARIA. The inputs are .vh rather than
      display:none so they keep focus, and the labels carry the styling.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <fieldset class="lada__units" data-lada-units hidden>
      <legend class="lada__units-legend">{{ legend }}</legend>
      <div class="lada__units-set">
        <input class="vh" type="radio" name="{{ uid }}-unit" id="{{ uid }}-unit-in"
               value="in" checked data-lada-unit>
        <label class="lada__units-opt" for="{{ uid }}-unit-in">{{ in_label }}</label>
        <input class="vh" type="radio" name="{{ uid }}-unit" id="{{ uid }}-unit-cm"
               value="cm" data-lada-unit>
        <label class="lada__units-opt" for="{{ uid }}-unit-cm">{{ cm_label }}</label>
      </div>
    </fieldset>
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Six columns will not fit a 360px phone at a legible size, and shrinking
    the type below 13px to make them fit is the one thing the type scale
    forbids. So the table keeps its size and the container scrolls, with
    role="region" and tabindex="0" so the scroll is reachable from the
    keyboard, named by the caption it already has rather than by a second
    string nobody would remember to translate.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="lada__sizescroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-labelledby="{{ uid }}-cap">
    <table class="lada__sizes lada__sizes--wide">
      <caption class="lada__sizes-cap" id="{{ uid }}-cap" data-lada-caption>{{ cap_in }}</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          {%- for head in cols -%}
            <th scope="col">{{ head | strip }}</th>
          {%- endfor -%}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {%- for row in row_list -%}
          {%- assign cells = row | split: ',' -%}
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">{{ cells[0] | strip }}</th>
            {%- comment -%}
              The loop runs to the number of HEADERS, not the number of
              cells in the row, so a row the client has half filled still
              produces a full-width, correctly aligned row. A missing cell
              draws a short hairline through CSS rather than a character,
              so a screen reader hears an empty cell instead of "dash".
            {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- for i in (1..last_col) -%}
              {%- assign v = cells[i] | strip -%}
              {%- if v == blank -%}
                <td class="is-empty"></td>
              {%- else -%}
                <td data-lada-in="{{ v | escape }}">{{ v }}</td>
              {%- endif -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
          </tr>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  {%- if note != blank -%}
    <p class="lada__sizes-note">{{ note }}</p>
  {%- endif -%}
</div>
